testerChainT -- SEQ 427 R0 (2026-10-26): tester's instruction chain against kant's own value for the same expression.

This file is not run alone. pop.sh builds one input per row: Tony's LIVE IncantForms/WorkingOn/tester up to the end of
its define block (the last line `    ;` before the first `stop();`), then everything above this file's `stop();`, then the
row. One process per row, so a crash costs only its own row. COUPLED to tester's shape: if tester's define stops ending in
a `    ;` line before its first `stop();`, pop.sh's assembly breaks and says so.

Each row prints kant's value first (the statement run on the interpreted road), then the chain's (xpListTest builds it,
testOP runs it). Modes: v = the expression's value; t = its truth (`if EX; kt = 1;` -- a false compare read as a value
prints the tag kv, bear-trap 26); s = a statement, righty read afterwards.
Measured on the way: kant's `||` yields a TRUTH, so `righty = 42 || qb > qc` leaves righty 1, not 42. TC-4 agrees today
by coincidence; TC-4b (`righty = 0 || ...`) is the row that tells the tier apart. TC-3b agrees by coincidence too
(`(qc > qa) + 1` is still truthy); it is the true sibling, and TC-3c is the discriminator.
TC-11..TC-15 (SEQ 434, pre-registering the tier split): kant's logic tier reads LEFT TO RIGHT as one tier -- TC-13
`qc > qa || qa > qc && qb > qc` is 0 in kant, where && binding tighter than || would give 1. TC-11 (logic left to right)
and TC-12 (a compare's right side carrying arithmetic, under ||) disagree today; TC-13, TC-14 (assign over logic over
compare) and TC-15 (arithmetic on a compare's LEFT) agree today and must still agree after.
TC-16 and TC-17 were REGISTERED AFTER THE BUILD (tier-split branch), their old values measured on the pre-split tester:
TC-16 a chained assign (old 2, kant 5; the split's nesting), TC-17 T || F && T (old 0, kant 1). TC-17 is the H7 row for
"an equal tier closes its own slot": TC-11 and TC-13 stay green with that close removed, because the half it loses does
not change their answer (H17).

